WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.100
Barbara Szeman, Produer of WILLA: So Corey
Mayne [Director] and I have done several films

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.530
and projects together in the past and we were
looking for any reason to get together and

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.369
create another one but to really make this
one on a whole other level and during the

00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:21.580
process we decided to adapt Stephen King’s
Willa.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.350
We came up with five steps to adapting a book
to a film.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.150
Corey Mayne, Director of WILLA: Number one
it would be to find a story that speaks to

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:34.379
you so I read WILLA while ago and I just really
responded to the general atmosphere that Stephen

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:38.200
King created with his characters and the setting.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.450
I really just wanted to take the audience
for a ride visually and emotionally and I

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.210
felt that WILLA was the best opportunity to
do that.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.310
Really you just have to rely on your instincts.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.310
What speaks to you?

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.750
What do you find yourself thinking about days
and weeks after you’ve read it?

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:57.840
Adrian Jaworski: As an actor I really get
a feel for the character’s growth from the

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:04.670
first minute all the way to the end and have
that nice arc of him kind of learning a lesson

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.299
in all of this.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:11.869
And not only that put putting myself in the
shoes of the character and asking myself questions

00:01:11.869 --> 00:01:15.079
and what I would do and how I would go about
doing it.

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:21.319
And just kind of moving forward with my co-stars
helping it out and driving a really good story

00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:22.939
forward.

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:24.869
Especially being an adaptation from Stephen
King.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:26.249
You want to definitely do that justice.

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:31.229
Keli Mayne, actress who plays WILLA: What
I really hope people take from this and what

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:37.029
I really think would be cool is if people
walked away from seeing this film with a different

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:43.970
perspective on how they see the world because
I actually found myself doing that after.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:56.139
I kind of living and thinking about the film
in the story and actually questioning if things

00:01:56.139 --> 00:01:57.979
around me are actually real.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:01.430
Barbara: So Steph number two is what is your
film?

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.430
Corey: Is it a short?

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:03.430
Is it a feature?

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.479
Is it a television series?

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:06.719
Is it a web series?

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:08.890
All of that is going to effect how you tell
your story.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.970
Barbara: If you only have 15 minutes to make
a film then how are you going to structure

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:13.970
that?

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:15.920
And how are you going to structure your story
to being 15 minutes?

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:22.780
Corey: Doing a short is kind of a fun opportunity
because of the limited time limit.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.950
It felt like everything was more urgent.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.230
What do you change, keep or toss?

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.660
Barbara: You kind of need to do a little bit
of all of that.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:38.310
Corey: That is going to be the step that you
are working on in pre-production, production

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:44.090
and post-production because I find once you
filter all of your elements through that entire

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:50.620
process then the fat naturally gets chopped
away, it falls off.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:57.050
Barbara: It’s not always about the story
in terms of what you decide to keep, change

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:05.550
or toss because sometimes there are elements
that you cannot bring into your story.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.750
In our case we were able to get access to
a train for our adaptation.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:16.320
But if you don’t have the budget to do so
or the ability to gain access to one you might

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.460
have to find a different route.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.860
So it’s important to see what you have available
to you.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:29.390
It’s important to look at your resources,
look at your crew, look at your cast and realize

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.550
what you have to work with.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:37.590
And those are going to make some changes into
the story and that’s how you can make it

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.590
your own.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:43.770
Corey: With the Stephen King adaptation, yes
that was an ongoing conversation and sometimes

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:50.520
it got controversial because you don’t want
to stray too far from the story’s original

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.520
intent.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.819
But you have some certain ideas that come
up or certain ideas that you want to try out

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:03.440
and naturally throughout the whole filming
process you have to also rely on the rest

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.420
of your crew and everyone else whose specialty
it is to handle their departments to let you

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:14.770
know if there is a better option to accomplish
something or a more efficient way to accomplish

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:23.319
something and I feel like we struck a really
good balance between efficiency and creativity

00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:26.860
and establishing the atmosphere that we wanted
and the style that we wanted.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:32.789
One thing that you do have to just learn to
live with is you can always work on something

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:33.789
to death.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:38.160
That’s where a hard deadline comes in handy
and especially working with a big company

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.520
like Deluxe.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.680
I don’t want to get on their nerves and
keep giving note after note.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.270
Basically it’s my responsibility to make
all of the best decisions in the moment and

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.700
any other decisions that I think of later
it should have been on me to have thought

00:04:55.700 --> 00:05:01.669
of them earlier (which probably comes from
experience) or maybe they are just not that

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:03.740
important if I didn’t think of it right
away.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.770
Barbara: Step four is how do you make it your
own?

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:12.879
That’s important because no two directors
are going to make the same film and with an

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:18.680
adaptation it’s important to stay true to
the original story but give it your own voice.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.539
Corey: That’s something you have to figure
out yourself.

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:24.879
Like I can’t tell you how to make it your
own, only you can really do that as a director

00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:33.789
so when I was making this story my own I really
just tried to interpret how I felt about the

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:39.530
story and what that feeling would look like
if that was a picture.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:46.150
Again that was due to in part because of our
amazing cinematographer and his team.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:50.729
Nick Szeman, Actor in WILLA: When it comes
to adapting a story I mean obviously you want

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:54.180
to stick with the source material as much
as possible because that’s what made it

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.629
great to begin with and that’s what made
it catch the attention of the people in charge

00:05:58.629 --> 00:05:59.629
of it.

00:05:59.629 --> 00:06:02.941
Corey and Barbara when they were writing it
WILLA stuck out to them because it was an

00:06:02.941 --> 00:06:06.430
incredible short story so obviously they want
to stick to that source material.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:14.069
So that being said as filmmakers they also
very, subtlety and carefully pick moments

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:17.400
to surprise the audience at the same time.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.689
The things they threw in there I think were
very tastefully done and I think things that

00:06:22.689 --> 00:06:26.919
will really stick with the audience and make
them think and reflect deeply afterwards.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:30.639
Corey: Sort of keep in touch with your feelings.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:35.259
Like you have to…it’s so hard to articulate
your own feelings.

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:36.470
At least it is for me.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.280
Some people are better at it than others.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.969
In order to be a normal person in society,
I think you just have to sort of ignore your

00:06:41.969 --> 00:06:48.960
feelings and just go through life or whatever
but being a director you really sort of have

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.389
to be in tune with what you’re feeling about
the subject matter you are dealing with or

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:57.789
about the performance you are seeing in the
monitor.

00:06:57.789 --> 00:07:00.379
Barbara: Step five is remember your audience.

00:07:00.379 --> 00:07:03.710
You need to keep in mind who is going to be
viewing your film.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:10.770
They are going to be fans of the book you
adapted it from and they want that same nostalgic

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.589
feeling when they original read it.

00:07:13.589 --> 00:07:16.699
So you have to be true to that, you have to
be true to them.

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:20.509
Corey: Because as much as you’re making
this film for yourself, you want other people

00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:24.229
to see it right?

00:07:24.229 --> 00:07:26.960
Our intent is to just obviously entertain
people.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.849
And I feel like we knew our audience because
we are a part of that whole horror community

00:07:32.849 --> 00:07:34.979
and Stephen King fan community.

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:38.069
We grew up watching these movies, we grew
up reading these stories.

00:07:38.069 --> 00:07:40.960
So we kept asking ourselves what would we
pay to see?

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.839
Horror fans in particular they can spot fakes
pretty easily.

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:54.479
I think they can have a good sense of whether
you are a fraud or a…they are highly critical

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:59.840
but some of the most diverse fans and best
fans out there.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.129
It can work either way.

00:08:01.129 --> 00:08:06.180
I’ve seen adaptations work that were completely
different from the book and I’ve seen adaptations

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.259
that have stuck to it religiously and it worked
as well.

00:08:10.259 --> 00:08:19.960
So it’s a gamble and you know…you don’t
really get into this business to play it safe

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:20.960
right?

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.879
Barbara: Our film was so special and unique.

00:08:22.879 --> 00:08:30.529
It was a small independent production, over
100 crew and we got so much support from our

00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:31.529
producers.

00:08:31.529 --> 00:08:40.030
We got support from Indiegogo, from everyone
in the grip department to catering to craft

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:43.170
services to hair and make-up, electrics.

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:50.200
We couldn’t have done this without every
one of you and post-production with Deluxe

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:51.200
as well.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.350
We’re so fortunate with this film and everyone
who has been a part of it.

